= Distribution shift =

== Overview ==
'''Distribution shift''' occurs when the data-generating distribution relevant at evaluation or deployment differs from the distribution represented by training data.

A model can generalize well within the training distribution and still fail after shift.

== Joint-distribution view ==
For inputs X and targets Y, training and target distributions can differ:

<pre>
P_train(X,Y) ≠ P_target(X,Y)
</pre>

Different assumptions about which parts change define different shift types.

== Covariate shift ==
Under covariate shift:

<pre>
P_train(X) ≠ P_target(X)
P_train(Y|X) = P_target(Y|X)
</pre>

Covariate shift is one special case, not the definition of all distribution shift.

== Other shifts ==
Related cases include label/prior shift, conditional/concept shift, domain shift, temporal drift, subgroup composition change, and changes in measurement processes.

Terminology varies across fields.

== Generalization ==
Ordinary iid test performance estimates performance under the sampled test distribution.

It does not by itself establish robustness to distribution shift.

== Causes ==
Shift can arise from:

* time;
* geography;
* policy changes;
* population changes;
* sensors or data pipelines;
* strategic behavior;
* intervention;
* deployment feedback;
* data-collection differences.

== Related concepts ==
[[Generalization (machine learning)]], [[Probability distribution]], [[Correlation]], [[Causality]], [[Spurious correlation]], and [[Shortcut learning]] help explain why shifted performance can fail.

[[Robustness]] concerns maintaining specified performance under perturbation, while [[Adaptation (systems)]] concerns changing the system in response to altered conditions; both are relevant responses to distribution shift but are not synonyms for it.

== A changed population is not necessarily model drift ==
A shift can arise when the population being sampled changes, when measurement hardware or annotation practice changes, or when the target relationship itself changes. A lower test score after deployment is evidence of performance change, but it does not identify the cause without analysis of the population, outcome definition, sensors and deployment process.

== Evaluation under shift ==
Tests designed for one source population may not predict target-domain risk. A robust evaluation can separate sites, time periods or meaningful subgroups, preserve the target distribution definition and compare error and calibration under each condition. The same model may be stable for one metric and unreliable for another.

== Limits of correction ==
Importance weighting can sometimes help under covariate-shift assumptions when the relevant distributions overlap and the conditional target relationship stays stable. It is not a universal correction for changed labels, concepts, unobserved confounders or unsupported regions. A model's [[Related to::Generalization (machine learning)|generalization]] must be assessed relative to the distribution for which a claim is being made.

